================================================================================
PART 2: WHAT IS STATE IN REACT?
================================================================================

STATE DEFINITION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State in React is:

• Component Data
  - Information React preserves for a component

• Updatable Data
  - Can change over time through a state setter

• Used During Rendering
  - Current state helps determine the component's UI

• Component-Specific
  - Each component instance can have its own state

• Persistent Across Re-renders
  - State survives normal component re-renders

• Removed When Component Identity Is Removed
  - State doesn't normally survive permanent unmount/removal

• Reactive
  - State updates can cause React to render updated UI

• Managed with Hooks
  - useState is commonly used for local state in function components

• Enables Interactivity
  - Components can respond to user actions

• Different from Props
  - Props are received from the parent
  - State belongs to the component that declares it


================================================================================
STATE VS PROPS VS REGULAR VARIABLES
================================================================================

+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| ASPECT           | STATE                 | PROPS                | REGULAR VARIABLE     |
+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| Source           | Component             | Parent               | Current function     |
| Update           | State setter          | Parent provides new  | Direct assignment    |
| Child Mutability | Component updates     | Read-only to child   | Mutable if declared  |
|                  | its own state         |                      | with let             |
| Re-render        | Can trigger render    | New props can render | Does not itself      |
|                  |                       | component            | trigger render       |
| Persistence      | Across renders        | Supplied on renders  | Recreated per render |
| Scope            | Component instance    | Component input      | Function execution   |
| React Awareness  | Yes                   | Yes                  | No state tracking    |
| Typical Use      | Dynamic UI data       | Parent → Child data  | Temporary values     |
| UI Participation | Yes                  | Yes                  | Can be used during   |
|                  |                       |                      | current render       |
+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------+----------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A regular variable CAN affect JSX during the current render.

However:

Changing a regular variable does NOT tell React to render again.

Example:

let count = 0

count = count + 1

This changes the JavaScript variable, but does NOT create a React
state update.


================================================================================
PART 3: THE STATE LIFECYCLE
================================================================================

STATE LIFECYCLE OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State typically moves through these stages:

1. INITIALIZATION

   useState(initialValue)

        ↓

2. INITIAL RENDER

   Component renders with initial state

        ↓

3. COMPONENT COMMIT / DISPLAY

   React applies required DOM changes

        ↓

4. RUNNING

   Component waits for interactions

        ↓

5. STATE UPDATE REQUEST

   State setter is called

        ↓

6. RE-RENDER

   React renders component with updated state

        ↓

7. RECONCILIATION / COMMIT

   React determines and applies necessary DOM changes

        ↓

8. RUNNING AGAIN

   Component waits for another interaction

        ↓

9. COMPONENT REMOVED

   State associated with that component position/identity
   is normally discarded


================================================================================
STATE LIFECYCLE DIAGRAM
================================================================================

+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                    COMPONENT STATE LIFECYCLE                       |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                                    |
|  1. INITIALIZATION                                                 |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> useState(initialValue)                                 |
|                                                                    |
|  2. INITIAL RENDER                                                 |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> Component function executes                            |
|        +--> JSX is evaluated                                      |
|                                                                    |
|  3. COMMIT                                                        |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> Required DOM changes applied                           |
|        +--> User sees initial UI                                   |
|                                                                    |
|  4. RUNNING                                                       |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> User interacts with UI                                 |
|                                                                    |
|  5. STATE UPDATE                                                  |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> State setter called                                    |
|        +--> React queues/processes update                          |
|                                                                    |
|  6. RE-RENDER                                                     |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> Component function executes again                      |
|        +--> Updated state is available                             |
|                                                                    |
|  7. RECONCILIATION + COMMIT                                       |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> React determines UI changes                            |
|        +--> Necessary DOM updates applied                          |
|                                                                    |
|  8. RUNNING AGAIN                                                 |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> Ready for next interaction                             |
|                                                                    |
|  9. COMPONENT REMOVED                                             |
|        |                                                           |
|        +--> State associated with removed identity is discarded    |
|                                                                    |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+


STATE LIFECYCLE PHASES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+
| PHASE          | WHAT HAPPENS                         | STATE STATUS             |
+----------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+
| Initialization | useState initial value established   | Initial value            |
| Initial Render | Component function executes          | Available to render      |
| Commit         | Required DOM changes applied         | UI reflects state        |
| Running        | Component waits for interactions     | Preserved                |
| Update         | Setter queues/requests update        | Update pending/processed |
| Re-render      | Component executes with next state   | Updated state used       |
| Commit         | Required DOM changes applied         | UI synchronized          |
| Running Again  | Component waits again                | Preserved                |
| Removal        | Component identity disappears        | State normally discarded |
+----------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 4: HOW REACT DETECTS STATE CHANGES
================================================================================

IMPORTANT TECHNICAL IDEA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A useful beginner model is:

Old State
    ↓
State Setter
    ↓
Next State
    ↓
React Checks Whether State Is Effectively Unchanged
    ↓
Render Work As Needed


For useState, React can skip further rendering work when the state being
set is equivalent to the current state according to React's equality
behavior.

For objects and arrays, reference identity is therefore extremely important.


================================================================================
CHANGE DETECTION PROCESS
================================================================================

STEP 1: INITIAL RENDER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Component executes

• useState provides initial/current state

• JSX is evaluated

• React commits required DOM changes


STEP 2: USER INTERACTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• User clicks a button

or

• User types into an input

        ↓

Event handler executes


STEP 3: STATE SETTER CALLED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

setCount(count + 1)


React receives/queues the requested update.


STEP 4: STATE EQUALITY / IDENTITY MATTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Primitive example:

Current:

0

Requested:

1

These represent different state values.


Object example:

Current:

userObjectA

Requested:

userObjectB

If B is a newly created object, it has different identity.


STEP 5: RE-RENDER WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When React processes an effective state change:

• Component function executes again

• Current state for that render is available

• JSX is reevaluated


STEP 6: RECONCILIATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

React compares the newly produced UI description against the previous one
and determines what needs to change.


STEP 7: COMMIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Required DOM updates are applied.


STEP 8: UI UPDATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The browser displays the updated interface.


================================================================================
CHANGE DETECTION EXAMPLE
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function ChangeDetectionDemo() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  // ✓ Different number value

  const handleIncrement = () => {

    setCount(count + 1)
  }


  // Same state value

  const handleSameValue = () => {

    setCount(count)
  }


  const [user, setUser] = useState({
    name: 'John'
  })


  // ✓ Creates a new object

  const handleNameChange = (newName) => {

    setUser({
      ...user,
      name: newName
    })
  }


  // ❌ WRONG - Mutates current object

  const handleWrongObjectUpdate = (newName) => {

    user.name = newName

    setUser(user)

    // Existing object was mutated
    // Same object reference is passed back
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <h2>
        Change Detection Demo
      </h2>


      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleSameValue}>
        Set Same Value
      </button>


      <p>
        User: {user.name}
      </p>

      <button
        onClick={() =>
          handleNameChange('Jane')
        }
      >
        Change Name - Correct
      </button>

      <button
        onClick={() =>
          handleWrongObjectUpdate('Jane')
        }
      >
        Change Name - Wrong
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


================================================================================
STATE VALUE / REFERENCE EXAMPLES
================================================================================

+--------------------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+
| SCENARIO                 | CURRENT           | NEXT              | EFFECTIVE CHANGE |
+--------------------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+
| Increment number         | 0                 | 1                 | Yes              |
| Same number              | 0                 | 0                 | No               |
| Different object         | object A          | object B          | Yes identity     |
| Same object reused       | object A          | object A          | Same identity    |
| New array                | array A           | array B           | Yes identity     |
| Same mutated array       | array A           | array A           | Same identity    |
| String changed           | "hello"           | "world"           | Yes              |
| Same string              | "hello"           | "hello"           | No               |
| Boolean toggled          | false             | true              | Yes              |
| Same boolean             | false             | false             | No               |
+--------------------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For objects:

{ count: 1 }

and another:

{ count: 1 }

contain the same-looking data but are still two separate objects.


================================================================================
PART 5: STATE IMMUTABILITY AND REFERENCES
================================================================================

UNDERSTANDING IMMUTABILITY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Immutability means:

• Do Not Modify Existing State
  - Treat current state as read-only

• Create the Next Value
  - Create a replacement object/array when data changes

• Preserve Previous State
  - Previous state isn't overwritten by mutation

• Create New References
  - Updated objects and arrays have new identity

• Prevent Unexpected Side Effects
  - Shared references aren't unexpectedly modified

• Improve Debugging
  - Previous and next values are easier to distinguish

• Support Optimization
  - Reference equality becomes useful


================================================================================
REFERENCE VS VALUE
================================================================================

PRIMITIVE TYPES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const a = 5
const b = 5

console.log(a === b)

// true


const c = 'hello'
const d = 'hello'

console.log(c === d)

// true


OBJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const obj1 = {
  name: 'John'
}


const obj2 = {
  name: 'John'
}


console.log(obj1 === obj2)

// false


Why?

They are two separate objects.


ARRAYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const arr1 = [1, 2, 3]

const arr2 = [1, 2, 3]


console.log(arr1 === arr2)

// false


Even though their elements look identical,
they are separate arrays.


SAME OBJECT REFERENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const obj3 = obj1


console.log(obj1 === obj3)

// true


obj1 and obj3 point to the same object.


================================================================================
IMMUTABILITY IN STATE UPDATES
================================================================================

OBJECT - WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [user, setUser] = useState({
  name: 'John',
  age: 30
})


const updateName = (newName) => {

  user.name = newName

  setUser(user)
}


Problem:

Existing state object is mutated and then reused.


OBJECT - CORRECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const updateName = (newName) => {

  setUser({
    ...user,
    name: newName
  })
}


Result:

Existing Object
      ↓
Copy Properties
      ↓
Replace name
      ↓
New Object
      ↓
setUser()


================================================================================
ARRAY - WRONG
================================================================================

const [items, setItems] =
  useState([1, 2, 3])


const addItem = (newItem) => {

  items.push(newItem)

  setItems(items)
}


Problem:

push() modifies the existing array.


ARRAY - CORRECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const addItem = (newItem) => {

  setItems([
    ...items,
    newItem
  ])
}


Result:

Existing Array
      ↓
Copy Existing Elements
      ↓
Add New Item
      ↓
New Array
      ↓
setItems()


================================================================================
IMMUTABILITY PATTERNS
================================================================================

+------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------------+
| DATA/OPERATION   | WRONG                     | CORRECT                           |
+------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------------+
| Simple Value     | Regular variable change   | setState(newValue)                |
| Object           | state.prop = value        | {...state, prop: value}           |
| Nested Object    | state.a.b = value         | Copy each changed level           |
| Array Add        | array.push(item)          | [...array, item]                  |
| Array Remove     | array.splice(...)         | array.filter(...)                 |
| Array Update     | item.prop = value         | array.map(...)                    |
| Array Sort       | array.sort() directly     | [...array].sort()                 |
| Array Reverse    | array.reverse() directly  | [...array].reverse()              |
| Toggle Boolean   | Direct reassignment       | setFlag(prev => !prev)            |
| Increment        | Direct increment          | setCount(prev => prev + 1)        |
| Decrement        | Direct decrement          | setCount(prev => prev - 1)        |
+------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 6: STATE AND RE-RENDERING
================================================================================

WHAT CAN CAUSE A COMPONENT TO RENDER AGAIN?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Common reasons include:

• Its State Is Updated

• Its Parent Renders
  - Children are normally evaluated again unless an optimization prevents it

• Consumed Context Changes

• Its Position/Identity Changes

• Its key Changes
  - A changed key can cause React to treat it as a different component,
    resetting its state


PROPS NOTE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A component can receive new props because its parent renders.

Props themselves are component inputs.

A child render can be skipped in specific optimized scenarios, such as
when using React.memo and the relevant inputs are considered unchanged.


================================================================================
WHAT DOES NOT BY ITSELF REQUEST A RE-RENDER?
================================================================================

• Changing a regular local variable

• Directly mutating an object

• Directly mutating an array

• Simply executing an event handler

• setTimeout firing without a state/update action

• Receiving an API result without storing/using it in reactive state


================================================================================
RE-RENDER PROCESS
================================================================================

1. STATE SETTER CALLED

   setState(nextValue)

          ↓

2. REACT QUEUES / PROCESSES UPDATE

          ↓

3. COMPONENT RENDER

   Component function executes again when render work is required

          ↓

4. JSX EVALUATED

   Current state is used

          ↓

5. RECONCILIATION

   React determines differences

          ↓

6. COMMIT

   Necessary DOM changes are applied

          ↓

7. USER SEES UPDATED UI


================================================================================
RE-RENDER EXAMPLE
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function RerenderDemo() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  const [name, setName] = useState('John')


  console.log(
    'Component rendered:',
    count,
    name
  )


  const handleIncrement = () => {

    console.log(
      'Before setter:',
      count
    )

    setCount(count + 1)

    console.log(
      'After setter in same handler:',
      count
    )

    // count is still the value from
    // this render's event-handler closure
  }


  const handleNameChange = (newName) => {

    setName(newName)
  }


  const handleSameValue = () => {

    setCount(count)

    // Requesting the same state value can be skipped
    // by React
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <h2>
        Re-render Demo
      </h2>

      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <p>
        Name: {name}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment Count
      </button>

      <button
        onClick={() =>
          handleNameChange('Jane')
        }
      >
        Change Name
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleSameValue}>
        Set Same Value
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


EXAMPLE FLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose count = 0.

User Clicks Increment
        ↓
handleIncrement executes
        ↓
Before setter: 0
        ↓
setCount(1)
        ↓
Current handler STILL sees count = 0
        ↓
React processes update
        ↓
New render receives count = 1
        ↓
UI displays 1


================================================================================
RENDER TRIGGERS / NON-TRIGGERS
================================================================================

+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------------+
| ACTION                   | CAN CAUSE RENDER?       | REASON                          |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------------+
| State changes            | Yes                     | Reactive state updated          |
| Same state requested     | Usually skipped         | State effectively unchanged     |
| Parent renders           | Normally yes for child  | Child evaluated with parent     |
| Context changes          | Yes for consumers       | Consumed context updated        |
| Regular variable change  | No by itself            | Not React state                 |
| Direct state mutation    | No update by itself     | Setter not properly used        |
| Event handler runs       | No by itself            | Needs reactive update           |
| setTimeout callback      | No by itself            | Needs reactive update           |
| API response arrives     | No by itself            | Needs state/store update        |
| User clicks button       | No by itself            | Handler must request update     |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 7: STATE PERSISTENCE AND COMPONENT INSTANCES
================================================================================

STATE PERSISTENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State is preserved:

• Across Re-renders

• While React Preserves the Component at the Same Position/Identity

• During Parent Re-renders when the Child Identity Remains Stable

State is normally reset when:

• Component Is Removed

• Component Identity Changes

• key Changes

• Application/Page Reloads


================================================================================
STATE ISOLATION
================================================================================

State is isolated between component instances.

Example:

<Counter />
<Counter />
<Counter />


These are three Counter instances.

Each has its own count state.


================================================================================
COMPONENT INSTANCE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  return (
    <div>

      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <button
        onClick={() =>
          setCount(previous =>
            previous + 1
          )
        }
      >
        Increment
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


export default function App() {

  return (
    <div>

      <h1>
        Multiple Component Instances
      </h1>


      <div>

        <h2>Counter 1</h2>

        <Counter />

      </div>


      <div>

        <h2>Counter 2</h2>

        <Counter />

      </div>


      <div>

        <h2>Counter 3</h2>

        <Counter />

      </div>

    </div>
  )
}


RESULT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Counter 1:

Count: 0


Counter 2:

Count: 0


Counter 3:

Count: 0


Click Increment on Counter 1:

Counter 1:

Count: 1


Counter 2:

Count: 0


Counter 3:

Count: 0


WHY?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each rendered Counter instance has independent state.


================================================================================
STATE PERSISTENCE TABLE
================================================================================

+-------------------------+------------------+------------------------------------+
| SCENARIO                | STATE PRESERVED? | REASON                             |
+-------------------------+------------------+------------------------------------+
| Component re-renders    | Yes              | Same component identity            |
| State setter called     | Yes              | State updated, not discarded        |
| Props change            | Normally yes     | Same component identity             |
| Parent re-renders       | Normally yes     | Child remains in same position      |
| Component removed       | No               | Component state discarded           |
| Component added again   | New state        | New component instance/identity     |
| Conditional removal     | No               | Component removed from tree         |
| List item removed       | No for item      | That component instance removed     |
| key changes             | No               | React treats it as new identity     |
| Browser refresh         | No by default    | JavaScript application restarts     |
+-------------------------+------------------+------------------------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State is tied to a component's position/identity in the rendered tree.

This is more precise than simply saying:

"State belongs to the function."


================================================================================
PART 8: STATE AND PERFORMANCE
================================================================================

HOW STATE CAN AFFECT PERFORMANCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State management can affect performance through:

• Render Scope
  - State placed high in the component tree may cause more components
    to participate in renders

• Update Frequency
  - Frequently changing state creates more render work

• Component Design
  - Smaller component boundaries can limit affected areas

• State Structure
  - Unnecessarily complicated state makes updates harder

• Expensive Calculations
  - Heavy calculations during rendering can become costly

• Large Lists
  - Rendering many items may require additional optimization


================================================================================
PERFORMANCE STRATEGIES
================================================================================

• Keep State Minimal
  - Don't store unnecessary or derived values

• Place State Near Where It's Needed
  - Avoid lifting state higher than necessary

• Use Immutable Updates
  - Creates predictable update behavior

• Avoid Unnecessary State Updates
  - Don't call setters when nothing needs to change

• Use React.memo When Appropriate
  - Can skip child rendering when relevant props are unchanged

• Use useMemo When Appropriate
  - Can memoize expensive calculations

• Use useCallback When Referential Stability Matters
  - Can preserve a function reference across renders when useful

• Use Functional State Updates
  - Helpful when next state depends on previous state

• Measure Before Optimizing
  - Don't add memoization everywhere automatically


================================================================================
PERFORMANCE IMPACT TABLE
================================================================================

+--------------------------+-------------------+------------------------------------+
| PRACTICE                 | GENERAL IMPACT    | WHY                                |
+--------------------------+-------------------+------------------------------------+
| Minimal state            | Helpful           | Less duplicated state              |
| Excessive state          | Can be harmful    | More synchronization complexity     |
| State too high in tree   | Can be harmful    | Larger render scope                 |
| Immutable updates        | Helpful           | Predictable references              |
| Direct mutation          | Harmful pattern   | Breaks state assumptions            |
| Unnecessary updates      | Can be harmful    | Extra render work                   |
| Batched updates          | Helpful           | Multiple updates coordinated        |
| Deeply nested state      | Harder to manage  | More complex immutable updates      |
| Measured memoization     | Can help          | Avoids repeated expensive work      |
+--------------------------+-------------------+------------------------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do NOT assume:

"Every setState call = expensive DOM update."

React first renders/reconciles and only commits DOM changes that are needed.


