import { useState } from 'react'


export default function StateFlowDemo() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  console.log(
    'Component rendered, count:',
    count
  )


  const handleClick = () => {

    console.log(
      'Step 1: Button clicked'
    )


    console.log(
      'Step 2: Count for this render:',
      count
    )


    console.log(
      'Step 3: Requesting next value:',
      count + 1
    )


    setCount(count + 1)


    console.log(
      'Step 4: State update requested'
    )


    console.log(
      'Step 5: Current handler still sees:',
      count
    )
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <h2>
        State Flow Demo
      </h2>

      <p>
        Current Count: {count}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleClick}>
        Click to Increment
      </button>

      <p>
        Open console to observe the flow.
      </p>

    </div>
  )
}


EXAMPLE CONSOLE FLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Assume count starts at 0:

Step 1: Button clicked

Step 2: Count for this render: 0

Step 3: Requesting next value: 1

Step 4: State update requested

Step 5: Current handler still sees: 0

Component rendered, count: 1


WHY DOES THE HANDLER STILL SEE 0?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because event handlers capture the state values from the render in which
they were created.

Calling:

setCount(1)

does not rewrite the count variable inside the currently executing handler.

The next render receives the updated state.


================================================================================
PART 10: STATE UPDATES, SNAPSHOTS AND BATCHING
================================================================================

IMPORTANT MENTAL MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of thinking:

"setState immediately changes my variable"

think:

"setState requests/queues the next state for React to process."


STATE AS A SNAPSHOT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each render receives a snapshot of state.

Example:

Render 1:

count = 0

Handler created using count = 0


User clicks:

setCount(1)


The existing handler still has:

count = 0


React renders again:

Render 2:

count = 1


================================================================================
STATE UPDATE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function StateUpdateDemo() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  const handleClick = () => {

    console.log(
      'Before setter:',
      count
    )


    setCount(count + 1)


    console.log(
      'After setter:',
      count
    )
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleClick}>
        Increment
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


CONSOLE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before setter: 0

After setter: 0


Then React processes the update and a later render uses:

count = 1


================================================================================
STATE TIMING TABLE
================================================================================

+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| LOCATION                          | VALUE YOU TYPICALLY OBSERVE               |
+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| Before setter in current handler  | Current render's state                   |
| After setter in same handler      | Same current render's state              |
| Next render                       | Updated state                            |
| Functional updater argument       | Previous state for that queued update    |
+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Saying:

"setState is asynchronous"

is a common beginner shorthand.

A more accurate mental model is:

• State values are snapshots for each render

• Calling the setter queues/requests an update

• React processes queued updates

• A later render receives the resulting state


================================================================================
BATCHING
================================================================================

React can batch multiple state updates so they can be processed together.


EXAMPLE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function BatchingDemo() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  const [name, setName] = useState('John')


  const handleMultipleUpdates = () => {

    setCount(count + 1)

    setName('Jane')

    setCount(count + 2)
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <p>
        Name: {name}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleMultipleUpdates}>
        Multiple Updates
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


WITH count = 0:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Inside this handler:

count = 0


Therefore:

setCount(count + 1)

means:

setCount(1)


and:

setCount(count + 2)

means:

setCount(2)


After React processes the queued replacement updates, the resulting count
in this example is:

2


name becomes:

Jane


================================================================================
IMPORTANT BATCHING DIFFERENCE
================================================================================

REPLACEMENT STYLE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(count + 1)
setCount(count + 1)
setCount(count + 1)


If count is 0 in this render, each call effectively requests:

setCount(1)


Result:

1


FUNCTIONAL UPDATE STYLE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(count => count + 1)

setCount(count => count + 1)

setCount(count => count + 1)


React processes each updater sequentially:

0
 ↓ +1
1
 ↓ +1
2
 ↓ +1
3


Result:

3


WHY FUNCTIONAL UPDATES MATTER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When the next value depends on the previous state:

Prefer:

setCount(previous =>
  previous + 1
)


This makes the dependency explicit and works correctly with queued updates.


