PART 11: SUMMARY AND KEY TAKEAWAYS
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'VE LEARNED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

✓ State stores component-specific information

✓ useState provides a state value and setter

✓ State persists across re-renders while component identity is preserved

✓ Each component instance can have independent state

✓ State should be treated as immutable

✓ Objects and arrays have reference identity

✓ Direct mutation should be avoided

✓ State setters request/queue updates

✓ Each render sees a snapshot of state

✓ React can batch multiple state updates

✓ Functional updates are important when the next state depends on previous state

✓ Component rendering and DOM updates are separate ideas

✓ React reconciles rendered output before committing DOM changes

✓ Same/equivalent state updates can be skipped

✓ State is normally reset when component identity is removed or changed


================================================================================
CRITICAL POINTS TO REMEMBER
================================================================================

• NEVER mutate state directly

• Use the state setter

• Treat state objects and arrays as read-only

• Create new objects and arrays for changes

• Use functional setters for previous-state-dependent updates

• State is a snapshot for a particular render

• Calling a setter does not rewrite the current handler's state variable

• State is associated with component identity/position

• Different component instances have independent local state

• Changing a key can reset component state

• Parent renders normally cause children to be evaluated again

• DOM changes happen only when React determines they are necessary

• Keep state minimal

• Don't store easily derived values unnecessarily


